@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Filtering-patterns a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Filtering patterns";
    ds:summary "This documentation outlines the various search and filter patterns that can be used on sites, apps and docs to help users narrow down datasets into relevant results. It ranges from simple search functions to complex query builders. The designer should select a search and filtering mechanism that considers screen real estate, dataset size, and user intent, as outlined in the document.";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "# **Specification**\n\n## Search \n\nSearch facilitates information retrieval from a dataset based on a user’s written search query. Types:\n\nTable 74\n\n​\n\nIf including search in your design, use this decision tree as a starting point.  Additional considerations are in the comparison table below. \n\n\n\n## Filtering pattern\n\nFilters help users refine a dataset based on parameters set by the user. Types: \n\nTable 75\n\n​\n\nUse this decision tree as a starting point for your design. Additional considerations are in the comparison table below. \n\n## \n\n## Filter option component types\n\nThese filter option components can be triggered by a button in a filter bar or by a singular filter button. These options will likely be expanded on in the coming cycles, as we may also add other input types such as date pickers and numerical inputs. \n\nTable 76\n\n​  \n\n\n## Comparison table\n\nTable 77\n\n\n\n## Layout options\n\nTable 78\n\n\n\n## System behaviour\n\n### **Data fetching**\n\nIn deciding whether to update data dynamically or to apply batch filtering, the following considerations should be made:​\n\n- **Live filtering**: Updates dataset immediately upon selection of filters. Appropriate for simple, short lists of filters and when performance would not be an issue. Not appropriate for multi-select filters where additional friction may be required - use per-filter fetching instead in this case.\n  - Note: Dynamic filters can be disorienting for users, please read the [accessibility guidelines](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13k8WUm2Ahn-w_LG66D-KhxVwxJlIwXUJjWjs-e1Xhq8/edit?tab=t.q7yulhuv2mvg#heading=h.y6bzj1gszlkm) if implementing this approach. \n- **Per-filter**: Updates when the user completes their selection within a filter category and clicks outside the dropdown area or clicks an “Apply” button for multi-select filters, or as soon as the user makes a selection for single-select filters. This is an intermediary solution between live and batch filtering.\n- **Batch filtering**: Updates the dataset only when the user clicks a global “Apply” button. Appropriate for complex and large datasets as it prevents the UI from switching while users make their selection, or when performance is a concern.\n\n### **Loading states**\n\nDepending on the amount of time taken to fetch results as a result of search and filtering, different types of user feedback should be provided:\n\n- If results can be surfaced in less than 1 second, do not add a loading state as users will not be able to pick up on what happened. \n- If it takes longer than 1 second, loading states should be used to provide feedback that a task is in progress ([NN Group](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/progress-indicators/)). Use the [processing button state](https://vanillaframework.io/docs/patterns/buttons#processing). \n- For anything that takes more than 5 seconds to load, do not use spinners as progress indicators as users cannot be sure if the system is still working and may abandon the task entirely. Consider using percentage-done progress indicators instead. \n\n## Filter selection indicators\n\nTable 79\n\n\n\n## Content guidelines\n\n### **Filter categories**\n\n- Only use filters for the most relevant categories that users need. Try to limit the number of filters so users aren’t overwhelmed.\n- Use descriptive category headings so users can predict what values are in the category and understand the differences between categories.\n- Categories should appear in order of most general to most specific. For example, filters should progress from product category, brand and color, price, size and weight.\n- Consider the order of the options within filter categories. Quantitative filters should be in ascending or descending order. Qualitative categories should be alphabetical or in order of relevance/popularity.\n\n### **Common copy for filter interactions**\n\n- **Filter by** label can be included next to the buttons, but isn’t required.\n- **Apply** button to trigger batch filtering.\n- **Clear all** to clear filters selected across categories.\n- **Reset all** to reset filters across categories to the default view, which may be pre-filtered. For example, on a “My requests” page, the default view has the filter requester = user applied, so resetting would display this pre-filtered view. \n- **Clear selection** to clear filters selected within a category\n- **Select all** to select all filters within a category.\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n## Accessibility\n\nFilters should adhere to the accessibility guidelines for the components used within the filters. For instance, if combo boxes are used within the filters, designers should follow the accessibility guidelines for combo boxes. \n\n​\n\nEnsure:\n\n- Filters are labeled.\n- All filter controls should be navigable with a keyboard. Users should be able to tab through options, use arrow keys to move between options, use the spacebar to select/deselect options, and use the enter key to trigger buttons.\n\n  \n\n\nWCAG’s [3.2.2 On Input (Level A)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/on-input) outlines that users should be warned if their context will change based on their input, ensuring that interactive content is predictable. Context changes include a page reload, navigation to a new window, moving focus to another component and any other change that disorients the user. If a context change occurs, ensure the design:\n\n- Includes [submit buttons](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G80) so the user has control in applying changes, or; \n- Includes[ written instructions](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G13) before the filters to describe what will happen before a change occurs. \n\n  \n\n\nIf a dynamic filter updates results on the same page, it does not count as a change of context. In this case, the screen should: \n\n- Communicate that the results have changed by including the number of results shown or updating the heading (e.g. <h2>Results (24)</h2>).\n- The focus stays on the filter the user last interacted with.\n\n  \n\n\n# **Spec History and Changelog**\n\nTable 80\n\n​";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
